"enText": "Ephesus (以弗所) is one of the best-preserved cities of the Roman East and a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Walking down Curetes Street (庫瑞忒斯大道) toward the Library of Celsus (塞爾蘇斯圖書館) shows Roman urban design (羅馬城市設計): a paved, sloping street lined with fountains and monuments that ends in a framed vista (端景). The library façade, built in the early second century AD, is a two-storey screen of paired columns and aediculae (壁龕框架), richly carved with Corinthian capitals (科林斯柱頭) and scroll ornament (渦卷紋). On the Temple of Hadrian (哈德良神廟), an arch springs from the entablature (柱頂楣構) between columns, a typically Roman variation on the Greek temple front. In the Terrace Houses (山坡宅邸), wealthy homes preserve floor mosaics (馬賽克地板) in geometric patterns and fragments of wall painting (壁畫). Together they show how Roman builders combined classical orders, ornament and engineering into theatrical urban scenery. The scale of the library façade is best understood with a person standing in front of it, which the photographs here allow.",
"zhText": "以弗所是羅馬東方保存最完整的城市之一（世界遺產）。沿著庫瑞忒斯大道往下走，盡頭就是塞爾蘇斯圖書館，示範羅馬城市設計的「端景」手法。圖書館立面是兩層成對柱與壁龕框架組成的屏幕，刻滿科林斯柱頭與渦卷紋；哈德良神廟在柱頂楣構中間拱起一道「敘利亞拱」，是羅馬人對希臘神廟正面的變奏。山坡宅邸保存了幾何馬賽克地板與壁畫殘片。站在圖書館前的人像照，最能看出立面的尺度。",
